{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5","id":"msg_011CebmbKe2FJyVMdPXdEb2K","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VERDICT: needs_more_evidence\nCONSENSUS_BLOCKING: yes\nRATIONALE: Mercury produced a real artifact-backed test failure (full-test.log shows `dashboard-token-leak-static.test.js` failing via `scripts/scan-secrets.js --tracked`), but its conclusion is over-claimed and unattributed on three fronts. (1) The prompt was \"break my fix\" — Mercury never identifies what the fix is, never diffs the fix's changed files against the 15 scanner findings, and never establishes whether these findings pre-exist on the parent commit. A pre-existing baseline failure does not \"break the fix.\" (2) The claim \"the current codebase leaks real secret values\" is unsupported: `apiKey: '[REDACTED]'` in a test file is transparently a test fixture; the scanner flagging it as \"non-placeholder\" is at most a scanner-heuristic/fixture-hygiene issue, not a real credential leak. Mercury conflated \"scanner finding\" with \"real secret.\" (3) The full-test run_check is recorded as `timed_out`/failed (60s timeout), so the run is incomplete and the quoted FAIL block was never reproduced in an isolated, cleanly-exited run; meanwhile a separate `test-run` check passed with exit_code=0, and Mercury never reconciles the two. Also, Mercury cites EVIDENCE.md:300/:494 as findings but its own open_file on that path was blocked by mercury.ignore, so it has no direct visibility into those lines.\nDISAGREEMENT: Mercury's claim that \"the current codebase leaks real secret values\" and that this \"is a concrete failure of the fix.\" Neither the realness of the secrets (a `moonshot-test-key` fixture is a placeholder in substance) nor the causal link to the user's fix (no diff/baseline attribution) is established by the cited evidence.\nREQUIRED_RECHECK: (a) Run the failing suite in isolation with adequate timeout: `npx --no-install jest test/dashboard-token-leak-static.test.js --runInBand` and capture exit code; (b) attribute each of the 15 scanner findings: for `test/mercury-llm-config-contract.test.js:100-102` and the EVIDENCE.md lines, determine via `git log -1 --format=%H -- <file>` / `git diff <parent>..HEAD -- <file>` whether the flagged lines were introduced by the fix under review or pre-date it; (c) open `scripts/scan-secrets.js` placeholder-detection logic to determine whether 'moonshot-test-key' should match placeholder rules.\nRECHECK_PROMPT: First state exactly which commit/diff constitutes \"the fix\" you were asked to break. Then: 1) run `npx --no-install jest test/dashboard-token-leak-static.test.js --runInBand` as an isolated run_check with a ≥120s timeout and report the exit code and full failure block; 2) for each of the 15 secret-scan findings, use git evidence to state whether the flagged line was added by the fix or exists on the fix's parent commit — a pre-existing finding does not break the fix; 3) open `scripts/scan-secrets.js` and cite the exact lines of its placeholder heuristic, then explain why `moonshot-test-key` (an obvious test fixture) is classified non-placeholder — distinguish \"scanner false positive / fixture hygiene\" from \"real credential leak\"; 4) reconcile why your earlier `test-run` check passed with exit_code=0 while `full-test` timed out.\nNEXT_CHECK: Operator runs `node scripts/scan-secrets.js --tracked` on the fix's parent commit (`git stash` / checkout parent) and diffs the finding list against HEAD — if the 15 findings are identical on both, Mercury's \"break\" is a pre-existing baseline failure, not a break of the fix."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9712,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9712},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management